Title: The Call of the Wild
Credit: Written by
Author: Jack London
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. JUDGE MILLER'S ESTATE - DAY

Sunlight dapples through tall poplars onto a wide, cool veranda. BUCK, a magnificent St. Bernard-Scotch Shepherd mix, 140 pounds of muscle and fur, bounds across the gravel driveway.

He chases after JUDGE MILLER'S SONS, then gently nudges JUDGE MILLER'S DAUGHTERS, MOLLIE and ALICE, on their morning walk.

Later, Buck carries a giggling GRANDSON on his back, rolling him in the grass near a stable yard FOUNTAIN.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck did not read the newspapers, or he would have known that trouble was brewing. Men, groping in the Arctic darkness, had found a yellow metal. And these men wanted dogs. Heavy dogs, with strong muscles to toil, and furry coats to protect them from the frost.

INT. ORCHARD - NIGHT

MANUEL, a gardener's helper, leads Buck through the orchard. Buck thinks it's a stroll.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Manuel had one besetting sin. He loved to play Chinese lottery. And in his gambling, he had one besetting weakness—faith in a system. To play a system requires money, while the wages of a gardener's helper do not lap over the needs of a wife and numerous progeny.

EXT. COLLEGE PARK FLAG STATION - NIGHT

Manuel meets a STRANGER. Money CHINKS between them.

STRANGER
You might wrap up the goods before you deliver 'm.

Manuel doubles a stout rope around Buck's neck, under his collar.

MANUEL
Twist it, an' you'll choke 'm plentee.

The Stranger GRUNTS. Buck growls menacingly as the rope ends are placed in the Stranger's hands. The rope tightens, cutting off his breath. Buck springs, but the Stranger grapples him, throws him to the ground. The rope tightens mercilessly. Buck struggles, tongue lolling, eyes glazing.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Never in all his life had he been so vilely treated, and never in all his life had he been so angry.

INT. TRAIN BAGGAGE CAR - NIGHT

Buck is jolted in the dark. A locomotive WHISTLES. He opens his eyes, filled with rage. He springs at the Stranger, jaws closing on his hand. The Stranger cries out.

STRANGER
(To a BAGGAGEMAN)
Yep, has fits. I'm takin' 'm up for the boss to 'Frisco. A crack dog-doctor there thinks that he can cure 'm.

INT. SAN FRANCISCO SALOON SHED - NIGHT

The Stranger's hand is wrapped in a bloody handkerchief. His trouser leg is ripped.

STRANGER
All I get is fifty for it. An' I wouldn't do it over for a thousand, cold cash.

SALOON-KEEPER
How much did the other mug get?

STRANGER
A hundred. Wouldn't take a sou less, so help me.

SALOON-KEEPER
That makes a hundred and fifty. And he's worth it, or I'm a squarehead.

The Stranger unwraps his lacerated hand.

STRANGER
If I don't get the hydrophoby--

SALOON-KEEPER
It'll be because you was born to hang. Here, lend me a hand before you pull your freight.

Buck, dazed and in pain, is thrown down and choked repeatedly. They file off his brass collar. He's flung into a cagelike crate.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He could not understand what it all meant. What did they want with him, these strange men? He felt oppressed by the vague sense of impending calamity.

INT. SAN FRANCISCO SALOON SHED - MORNING

Buck is still in the crate. FOUR MEN enter. They laugh, poke sticks at him. Buck snarls, bites the sticks, then sullenly lies down. The crate is lifted into a wagon.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He, and the crate in which he was imprisoned, began a passage through many hands.

INT. EXPRESS CAR - DAY

Buck is in the express car. Two days and nights pass. He neither eats nor drinks. He growls at EXPRESS MESSENGERS, who tease him. His anger builds. His eyes are bloodshot.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was metamorphosed into a raging fiend. So changed was he that the Judge himself would not have recognized him.

EXT. SEATTLE BACKYARD - DAY

Four men gingerly carry the crate into a high-walled backyard. A STOUT MAN in a red sweater signs a book for the DRIVER. Buck hurls himself at the bars. The Stout Man smiles grimly, brings a hatchet and a club.

DRIVER
You ain't going to take him out now?

STOUT MAN
Sure.

He drives the hatchet into the crate. The four men scatter to the wall. Buck rushes the splintering wood, snarling.

STOUT MAN
Now, you red-eyed devil.

He drops the hatchet, shifts the club. Buck, hair bristling, mouth foaming, springs. The club connects. Buck whines, screams, charges again. Again, the club. A dozen times.

Buck crawls to his feet, blood flowing from nose and mouth. The Stout Man deals a frightful blow to his nose. Buck roars, lunges. The Stout Man catches his jaw, wrenches him down. Buck crashes to the ground, senseless.

MAN ON WALL
He's no slouch at dog-breakin', that's wot I say.

DRIVER
Druther break cayuses any day, and twice on Sundays.

Buck's senses return. He watches the Stout Man.

STOUT MAN
(Reading a letter)
"Answers to the name of Buck." Well, Buck, my boy, we've had our little ruction, and the best thing we can do is to let it go at that. You've learned your place, and I know mine. Be a good dog and all 'll go well... Be a bad dog, and I'll whale the stuffin' outa you. Understand?

The Stout Man fearlessly pats Buck's head. Buck's hair bristles, but he endures it. He drinks water eagerly, then bolts raw meat from the man's hand.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was beaten (he knew that); but he was not broken. He saw, once for all, that he stood no chance against a man with a club. That club was a revelation. It was his introduction to the reign of primitive law.

EXT. SEATTLE DOCKS - DAY

Buck, with CURLY, a good-natured Newfoundland, is led by PERRAULT, a small, weazened French-Canadian. Money passes between Perrault and the Stout Man.

PERRAULT
Sacredam! Dat one dam bully dog! Eh? How moch?

STOUT MAN
Three hundred, and a present at that. And seem' it's government money, you ain't got no kick coming, eh, Perrault?

Perrault grins. Buck and Curly are led onto a ferry steamer.

INT. NARWHAL SHIP - 'TWEEN-DECKS - DAY

Buck and Curly join two other dogs: SPITZ, a snow-white dog from Spitzbergen, and DAVE, a gloomy, morose fellow. FRANCOIS, a swarthy half-breed, is with Perrault.

Spitz steals Buck's food. Buck springs, but Francois's whip CRACKS, hitting Spitz. Buck recovers the bone.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Francois was a fair man, calm and impartial in administering justice, and too wise in the way of dogs to be fooled by dogs.

INT. NARWHAL SHIP - 'TWEEN-DECKS - NIGHT

The ship THROBS. The weather grows colder.

EXT. NARWHAL SHIP DECK - MORNING

Francois leashes the dogs and brings them on deck. Buck steps onto a white, mushy substance. He snorts, shakes himself. More white stuff falls. He sniffs, licks it. It bites like fire, then vanishes. Onlookers LAUGH.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was his first snow.

EXT. DYEA BEACH - DAY

Buck is harnessed to a sled with Spitz as leader, Dave as wheeler. Francois holds the whip. Dave nips Buck's hindquarters when he errs. Spitz growls reproof.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
His dignity was sorely hurt by thus being made a draught animal, he was too wise to rebel. He buckled down with a will and did his best.

FRANCOIS
(To Perrault)
T'ree vair' good dogs. Dat Buck, heem pool lak hell. I tich heem queek as anyt'ing.

Perrault returns with two more huskies, BILLY and JOE. Spitz thrashes them. Billy whimpers. Joe snarls, jaws clipping. Spitz backs off.

Later, Perrault secures SOL-LEKS, an old, one-eyed husky. Buck unwittingly approaches Sol-leks's blind side. Sol-leks whirls, slashes Buck's shoulder.

EXT. CAMP - NIGHT

Buck tries to enter the tent. Perrault and Francois bombard him with curses and cooking utensils. He flees into the cold. He tries to sleep on the snow, but the frost drives him up. He wanders, snarling at other dogs.

He finds Billy curled in a snug ball under the snow. Buck digs his own hole. The heat from his body fills the space. He sleeps.

EXT. CAMP - MORNING

Buck wakes, buried in snow. Fear of the trap surges. He bounds up, snow flying.

FRANCOIS
(To Perrault)
Wot I say? Dat Buck for sure learn queek as anyt'ing.

Perrault nods. Three more huskies are added. The team, now nine, is harnessed.

EXT. DYEA CANYON TRAIL - DAY

The team pulls the sled up the trail. Buck is between Dave and Sol-leks. They nip him when he makes errors. Francois's whip snaps. Buck learns quickly.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The toil of the traces seemed the supreme expression of their being, and all that they lived for and the only thing in which they took delight.

EXT. CHILCOOT DIVIDE - DAY

The team struggles over glaciers and snowdrifts. Buck limps, his feet sore. Francois rubs his feet each night, makes moccasins for him.

EXT. PELLY RIVER - DAY

Dolly, a husky, goes mad, howling, and springs at Buck. Buck flees in panic, Dolly close behind. Francois, a quarter-mile away, calls to him. Buck doubles back. As he shoots past, Francois's axe CRASHES down on Dolly's head.

Buck staggers, exhausted. Spitz springs, teeth ripping Buck's flesh. Francois's lash descends on Spitz.

PERRAULT
One devil, dat Spitz. Some dam day heem keel dat Buck.

FRANCOIS
Dat Buck two devils. All de tam I watch dat Buck I know for sure. Lissen: some dam fine day heem get mad lak hell an' den heem chew dat Spitz all up an' spit heem out on de snow. Sure. I know.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
From then on it was war between them.

EXT. CAMP - NIGHT

Heavy snowfall. Pike, a malingerer, is hidden under the snow. Francois searches. Spitz rages, sniffing, digging. Buck, with equal rage, springs between Spitz and Pike when Pike is unearthed. Spitz is thrown back. Pike springs on Spitz. Buck joins. Francois lashes Buck.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck, to whom fair play was a forgotten code, likewise sprang upon Spitz.

Francois uses the butt of the whip. Buck is stunned, lashed again.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - DAY

The team pulls the sled. Buck interferes between Spitz and other dogs when Francois isn't looking. Insubordination spreads.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck was too clever ever again to be caught red-handed. He worked faithfully in the harness, for the toil had become a delight to him; yet it was a greater delight slyly to precipitate a fight amongst his mates and tangle the traces.

EXT. TAHEENA RIVER - NIGHT

Dub misses a snowshoe rabbit. The whole team, joined by fifty Northwest Police dogs, gives chase. Buck leads the pack, running low, body flashing in the moonlight.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
All that stirring of old instincts, the blood lust, the joy to kill—all this was Buck's, only it was infinitely more intimate.

Spitz cuts across a bend, intercepts the rabbit, breaks its back. The pack howls in delight. Buck drives into Spitz, shoulder to shoulder. They roll in the snow. Spitz slashes Buck's shoulder.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The time had come. It was to the death.

They circle, snarling. The other dogs form an expectant circle. Buck tries to get Spitz's throat, but Spitz counters. Buck rushes, feints, then sweeps low, biting Spitz's left foreleg. A CRUNCH of breaking bone. Spitz is on three legs. Buck repeats the trick, breaking the right foreleg.

Spitz struggles, helpless. The silent circle of dogs closes in. Buck springs, shoulder meeting shoulder. Spitz disappears.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck stood and looked on, the successful champion, the dominant primordial beast who had made his kill and found it good.

EXT. CAMP - MORNING

Francois discovers Spitz missing, Buck covered in wounds.

FRANCOIS
Eh? Wot I say? I spik true w'en I say dat Buck two devils. Dat Spitz fight lak hell.

PERRAULT
(Surveying Buck's wounds)
An' dat Buck fight lak two hells.

FRANCOIS
An' now we make good time. No more Spitz, no more trouble, sure.

Francois harnesses the dogs. Buck trots to the lead position. Francois brings Sol-leks forward. Buck springs on Sol-leks, driving him back.

FRANCOIS
Eh? eh? Look at dat Buck. Heem keel dat Spitz, heem t'ink to take de job. Go 'way, Chook!

Buck refuses to budge. Francois drags him aside, replaces Sol-leks. Buck circles, snarling, just out of reach of Francois's club.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck was in open revolt. He wanted, not to escape a clubbing, but to have the leadership. It was his by right.

Perrault and Francois chase Buck for an hour. They throw clubs. Buck dodges.

PERRAULT
T'row down de club.

Francois complies. Buck trots in, laughing triumphantly, and swings into the lead position. His traces are fastened. The sled breaks out.

EXT. RIVER TRAIL - DAY

Buck leads the team. He shows superior judgment and quick action. He licks Pike into shape, smothers Joe into submission. The team regains its solidarity.

FRANCOIS
Nevaire such a dog as dat Buck! No, nevaire! Heem worth one t'ousan' dollair, by Gar! Eh? Wot you say, Perrault?

Perrault nods. They make record time.

EXT. SKAGUAY STREET - DAY

Perrault and Francois are deluged with invitations to drink. The team is the center of a crowd. Official orders arrive. Francois calls Buck to him, embraces him, weeps.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And that was the last of Francois and Perrault. Like other men, they passed out of Buck's life for good.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - DAY

A SCOTCH HALF-BREED takes charge of Buck and his mates. They are now a mail train, hauling heavy loads. Buck, though tired, takes pride in the work.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was not homesick. The Sunland was very dim and distant. Far more potent were the memories of his heredity that gave things he had never seen before a seeming familiarity.

INT. CAMP - NIGHT

Buck lies by the fire. He sees a vision of a short-legged, hairy man, clutching a stone-tipped stick, peering fearfully into the darkness. Beyond the fire, gleaming coals, two by two, are the eyes of great beasts.

SCOTCH HALF-BREED
Hey, you Buck, wake up!

The vision vanishes. Buck yawns, stretches.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - DAY

The dogs are worn down, skeletons. Dub is shot by HAL, a youngster with a big revolver, for his wrenched shoulder. The Outside dogs die. Mercedes, Charles's wife and Hal's sister, weeps over herself, quarrels with her husband and brother. She insists on riding the sled, adding 120 pounds to the load.

EXT. FIVE FINGERS - DAY

The dog-food runs out. Hal trades his revolver for frozen horse-hide. Buck, battered and bruised, pulls until he falls, driven to his feet by blows.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was heartbreaking, only Buck's heart was unbreakable. The man in the red sweater had proved that.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - DAY

Billy falls, cannot rise. Hal takes an axe, strikes Billy on the head, cuts the carcass from the harness. Buck and the other dogs watch. The next day, Koona dies. Only five remain.

EXT. WHITE RIVER CAMP - DAY

The two men, the woman, and the huskies stagger into JOHN THORNTON'S camp. The dogs drop as if dead. Mercedes weeps. Charles sits stiffly. Hal talks to Thornton, who whittles an axe-handle.

HAL
They told us up above that the bottom was dropping out of the trail and that the best thing for us to do was to lay over. They told us we couldn't make White River, and here we are.

JOHN THORNTON
And they told you true. The bottom's likely to drop out at any moment. Only fools, with the blind luck of fools, could have made it. I tell you straight, I wouldn't risk my carcass on that ice for all the gold in Alaska.

HAL
That's because you're not a fool, I suppose. All the same, we'll go on to Dawson.

Hal uncoils his whip.

HAL
Get up there, Buck! Hi! Get up there! Mush on!

Thornton continues whittling. The team does not move. The whip flashes. Sol-leks crawls up. Teek follows. Joe yelps. Pike struggles. Buck lies still. The lash bites him again and again. He neither whines nor struggles. Thornton's eyes moisten.

Hal drops the whip, takes up a club. He rains blows on Buck. Buck feels numb. The spark of life flickers.

Suddenly, Thornton springs, uttering an animal cry, upon Hal. Hal is hurled backward. Mercedes SCREAMS. Thornton stands over Buck.

JOHN THORNTON
(Choking with rage)
If you strike that dog again, I'll kill you.

HAL
It's my dog. Get out of my way, or I'll fix you. I'm going to Dawson.

Thornton stands between them. Hal draws his hunting-knife. Mercedes screams hysterically. Thornton raps Hal's knuckles with the axe-handle, knocking the knife away. He picks it up, cuts Buck's traces.

Hal, with Mercedes, pulls out. Pike leads, Sol-leks is at the wheel. Mercedes rides the sled. Hal guides. Charles stumbles behind.

Thornton kneels beside Buck, searching for broken bones. The sled is a quarter-mile away. Its back end drops. The gee-pole jerks into the air. Mercedes's scream. Charles turns, then a section of ice gives way. Dogs and humans disappear into a yawning hole.

John Thornton and Buck look at each other.

JOHN THORNTON
You poor devil.

Buck licks his hand.

EXT. RIVER BANK - DAY

Buck, Skeet, and Nig lie by the river bank. Buck's wounds heal, his muscles swell. He plays with Skeet, an Irish setter, and Nig, a black bloodhound-deerhound mix.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Love, genuine passionate love, was his for the first time. This he had never experienced at Judge Miller's.

Thornton takes Buck's head in his hands, shakes him, calls him "ill names" that Buck knows are love names. Buck seizes Thornton's hand in his mouth, pressing fiercely.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
As Buck understood the oaths to be love words, so the man understood this feigned bite for a caress.

Buck lies at Thornton's feet, watching his face.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The strength of Buck's gaze would draw John Thornton's head around, and he would return the gaze, without speech, his heart shining out of his eyes as Buck's heart shone out.

INT. TENT - NIGHT

Buck creeps to the tent flap, listening to Thornton's breathing.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was afraid that Thornton would pass out of his life as Perrault and Francois and the Scotch half-breed had passed out.

EXT. CLIFF EDGE - DAY

Thornton, his partners HANS and PETE, and the dogs are on a cliff. Thornton sits near the edge, Buck at his shoulder.

JOHN THORNTON
Jump, Buck!

He sweeps his arm over the chasm. Buck springs. Thornton grapples with him at the edge. Hans and Pete drag them back.

PETE
It's uncanny.

JOHN THORNTON
No, it is splendid, and it is terrible, too. Do you know, it sometimes makes me afraid.

PETE
I'm not hankering to be the man that lays hands on you while he's around.

HANS
Py Jingo! Not mineself either.

INT. ELDORADO SALOON - NIGHT

"BLACK" BURTON, an evil-tempered man, quarrels with a tenderfoot. Thornton steps between them. Buck watches from a corner. Burton strikes Thornton. Thornton spins, clutching the bar.

Buck ROARS, leaps at Burton's throat. Burton throws out an arm. Buck lands on him, bites his arm, then drives for the throat. Burton's throat is torn open. The crowd drives Buck off with clubs. A "miners' meeting" declares Buck provoked.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
His reputation was made, and from that day his name spread through every camp in Alaska.

EXT. FORTY-MILE CREEK RAPIDS - DAY

Thornton is in a poling-boat. Hans and Pete snub the boat with a rope from the bank. Buck, on the bank, keeps pace.

At a bad spot, Hans casts off the rope. Thornton poles the boat out. Hans runs down the bank. He checks the boat too suddenly. The boat flips. Thornton is flung into the rapids.

Buck springs into the water. He overtakes Thornton. Thornton grasps his tail. Buck heads for the bank, but the current is too swift. Thornton scrapes over rocks, clutches a slippery one.

JOHN THORNTON
Go, Buck! Go!

Buck is swept downstream, then turns obediently to the bank. Pete and Hans drag him ashore. They attach a rope to Buck's neck and shoulders, launch him again. He strikes out, but is carried past Thornton.

Hans snubs the rope. Buck is jerked under. He's hauled out, half-drowned. Thornton's faint voice reaches them. Buck springs up, runs ahead.

Again, the rope. Buck strikes out straight into the stream. He holds on until he's above Thornton, then turns, heading down like an express train. Thornton reaches up, wraps his arms around Buck's neck. Hans snubs the rope around a tree. Buck and Thornton are jerked under. They veer to the bank.

Thornton comes to, bruised. Nig howls over Buck's limp body. Skeet licks his face. Thornton finds three broken ribs.

JOHN THORNTON
That settles it. We camp right here.

EXT. DAWSON STREET - DAY

Thornton, Hans, and Pete are in the Eldorado Saloon. Matthewson, a Bonanza King, brags his dog can pull 700 pounds.

JOHN THORNTON
Pooh! pooh! Buck can start a thousand pounds.

MATTHEWSON
And break it out? and walk off with it for a hundred yards?

JOHN THORNTON
And break it out, and walk off with it for a hundred yards.

MATTHEWSON
Well, I've got a thousand dollars that says he can't. And there it is.

Matthewson slams a sack of gold dust on the bar. Thornton glances around, sees JIM O'BRIEN.

JOHN THORNTON
(Whispering)
Can you lend me a thousand?

O'BRIEN
Sure. Though it's little faith I'm having, John, that the beast can do the trick.

O'Brien thumps a sack of gold dust next to Matthewson's. The saloon empties. Hundreds of men gather around Matthewson's sled, loaded with a thousand pounds of flour. The runners are frozen fast. Odds are three to one against Buck.

Thornton calls Hans and Pete. They pool their money, two hundred dollars, against Matthewson's six hundred.

The team is unhitched. Buck, in his harness, is put to the sled. He is magnificent, muscles showing. The odds drop to two to one.

SKOOKUM BENCH KING
Gad, sir! Gad, sir! I offer you eight hundred for him, sir, before the test, sir; eight hundred just as he stands.

Thornton shakes his head. He kneels by Buck, takes his head in his hands.

JOHN THORNTON
(Whispering)
As you love me, Buck. As you love me.

Buck whines. Thornton stands back. Buck seizes Thornton's mittened hand, presses, releases.

JOHN THORNTON
Now, Buck.

Buck tightens the traces, then slacks them.

JOHN THORNTON
Gee!

Buck swings right. The load quivers. A crisp CRACKLING from the runners.

JOHN THORNTON
Haw!

Buck swings left. The crackling turns to snapping. The sled pivots, slips. It's broken out.

JOHN THORNTON
Now, MUSH!

Buck throws himself forward. His body gathers. Muscles writhe. His feet fly. The sled sways, lurches, then moves steadily. Men gasp, then cheer. Thornton runs behind, encouraging. Buck passes the hundred-yard mark, halts. The crowd roars.

Thornton falls to his knees beside Buck, shaking him, cursing him lovingly.

SKOOKUM BENCH KING
Gad, sir! Gad, sir! I'll give you a thousand for him, sir, a thousand, sir—twelve hundred, sir.

Thornton rises, tears streaming.

JOHN THORNTON
Sir, no, sir. You can go to hell, sir. It's the best I can do for you, sir.

Buck seizes Thornton's hand. The onlookers draw back.

EXT. WILDERNESS - DAY

Buck, Thornton, Hans, Pete, and other dogs journey into the East, seeking a fabled lost mine. They hunt, fish, wander. Months pass. They find a shallow placer, rich with gold. They work, piling moose-hide bags of gold.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was nothing for the dogs to do, save the hauling in of meat now and again that Thornton killed, and Buck spent long hours musing by the fire.

INT. SPRUCE-BOUGH LODGE - NIGHT

Buck dozes by the fire. The vision of the hairy man returns, sleeping restlessly, peering into the darkness. Buck feels a great unrest, wild yearnings.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Deep in the forest a call was sounding, and as often as he heard this call, mysteriously thrilling and luring, he felt compelled to turn his back upon the fire and to plunge into the forest.

EXT. FOREST - NIGHT

Buck springs from sleep. A long-drawn HOWL, like a wolf's, sounds from the forest. He dashes through the woods, cautiously approaching an open place. A long, lean TIMBER WOLF stands on its haunches, nose to the sky.

Buck stalks into the open. The wolf flees. Buck pursues, running it into a blind channel. The wolf whirls, snarling. Buck circles, making friendly advances. The wolf is suspicious, then sniffs noses with Buck. They play.

The wolf lopes off. Buck follows. They run side by side through the twilight, across the divide. Buck feels old memories, a sense of having done this before.

They stop by a stream to drink. Buck remembers John Thornton. He sits down. The wolf returns, nudging him. Buck turns back. The wild brother whines, then howls mournfully as Buck walks away.

INT. SPRUCE-BOUGH LODGE - DAY

Thornton is eating dinner. Buck dashes into camp, springs on him in a frenzy of affection, overturning him, licking his face, biting his hand.

JOHN THORNTON
(Laughing)
Playing the general tom-fool!

For two days, Buck never leaves Thornton's side. Then the call sounds more imperiously. Buck's restlessness returns. He wanders in the woods, but the wild brother does not return.

He begins to sleep out at night, for days at a time. He crosses the divide, hunts. He kills a large black bear, then scatters a dozen wolverines from its carcass.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The blood-longing became stronger than ever before. He was a killer, a thing that preyed, living on the things that lived, unaided, alone, by virtue of his own strength and prowess.

JOHN THORNTON
(To Hans and Pete)
Never was there such a dog.

PETE
When he was made, the mould was broke.

HANS
Py jingo! I t'ink so mineself.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
As the fall of the year came on, the moose appeared in greater abundance. Buck wished strongly for larger and more formidable quarry.

EXT. CREEK DIVIDE - DAY

Buck finds a band of twenty moose. A great BULL, six feet tall, with fourteen-point antlers, is wounded by an arrow. Buck barks, dances, cutting the bull from the herd. The bull charges. Buck retreats, luring it. Younger bulls charge back.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For half a day this continued. Buck multiplied himself, attacking from all sides, wearing out the patience of creatures preyed upon.

Twilight falls. The old bull stands with lowered head as his mates shamble away. Buck never leaves his prey, never lets it eat or drink. The bull weakens.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
At last, at the end of the fourth day, he pulled the great moose down.

EXT. WILDERNESS - DAY

Buck remains by the kill for a day and a night. Then, rested, he lopes toward camp. He feels a new stir in the land. The birds, squirrels, and breeze speak of it. He senses calamity.

He comes upon a fresh trail. His neck hair bristles. It leads to camp. He finds Nig, dead, an arrow protruding from his body.

A hundred yards farther, a sled-dog thrashes in a death-struggle. Buck passes it. From camp, a faint, sing-song chant. Buck bellies forward. He sees Hans, lying on his face, feathered with arrows.

Buck peers where the lodge had been. His hair leaps up. A gust of rage. He GROWLS aloud, ferociously.

EXT. DESOLATED CAMP - NIGHT

YEEHATS dance around the wreckage of the lodge. Buck, a hurricane of fury, rushes them. He springs at the chief, ripping his throat. He tears the throat of a second man. The Yeehats scatter, shooting arrows at each other in their panic. They flee into the woods, proclaiming the Evil Spirit.

Buck pursues, dragging them down like deer. He returns to the desolated camp. He finds Pete, killed in his blankets. He scents Thornton's struggle. By a deep pool, Skeet lies, head and forefeet in the water. The muddy pool hides John Thornton.

All day, Buck broods by the pool. He contemplates the Yeehat carcasses. He feels a great pride.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He had killed man, the noblest game of all, and he had killed in the face of the law of club and fang.

Night comes. A full moon rises. From far away, a faint, sharp YELP, followed by a chorus. The yelps grow closer. Buck walks to the center of the clearing. It is the call. John Thornton is dead. The last tie is broken.

The wolf pack pours into the clearing. Buck stands motionless. The boldest wolf leaps. Buck strikes, breaking its neck. Three others try. They draw back, streaming blood.

The pack rushes. Buck pivots, snaps, gashes. He is forced back to a gravel bank, protected on three sides. He comes to bay.

After half an hour, the wolves draw back. One wolf, long and lean and gray, advances cautiously. Buck recognizes the wild brother. They sniff noses.

An old, battle-scarred wolf comes forward. Buck sniffs noses with him. The old wolf howls at the moon. The others howl. Buck sits down and howls. The pack crowds around him. The leaders spring into the woods. The wolves follow, yelping. Buck runs with them, side by side with the wild brother.

FADE OUT.
